WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.450
Speaker 1: All right.

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:06.720
When I make a mistake on the show and it's
more than just, you know, I just some kind

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.339
of minor thing, I want to make sure I come
to you and I say Sirs and M&amp;Ms.

00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:13.559
I made a mistake and I want to.

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:15.730
I want to correct the record.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:23.010
On Monday, I talked about the Glasgow Climate
Change Summit and the outrage over 400 private

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.700
jets flying there.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:32.430
And the irony and hypocrisy of Let's fix climate
and let's talk about it by flying in on our

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:37.150
private jets, which on a per person basis,
the carbon expenditures are off the charts.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.030
And I said a lot of different things in that
segment, including that rich people are going

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:45.950
to keep using private jets and that the economics
of private jets just make sense for CEOs and

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.710
other dignitaries when you account all different
factors.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:54.820
Eventually, private jets can run on renewable
fuels and they're not going away, and that

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.899
the real problem is that this is a distraction
sort of like change your straw and recycle

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:01.190
at home.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:06.869
I said, this is a distraction from the fact
that 100 companies are responsible for 71

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.490
percent of all emissions.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.170
That was not accurate, and I actually got
an email from Samuel about this.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.170
The article that I put up on the screen is
a Guardian article from four years ago.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:26.850
It's been widely shared, and it says 100 companies
are responsible for 71 percent of global emissions.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:32.560
A small number of fossil fuel producers and
their investors could hold the key to tackling

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.560
climate change.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.499
This this is not representative of the truth.

00:01:36.499 --> 00:01:37.499
OK.

00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:38.499
This has actually been looked at.

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:40.579
I'm glad to be corrected.

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:43.539
You can check out the Snopes article about
it.

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:46.409
It's misleading in the following sense.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:57.459
What the actual data is is that within the
universe of fossil fuel producers, 71 percent

00:01:57.459 --> 00:02:03.880
of the pollution from fossil fuel producers
comes from just 100 companies.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:09.740
It doesn't account for all sorts of other
types of companies that also pollute.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:16.069
So when I said 100 companies are responsible
for 71 percent of pollution, that's within

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:23.829
fossil fuel producers, coal gas drilling,
etc., it doesn't account for everything.

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:26.250
The analysis is still the same.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:33.980
The analysis is still there are corporations
that pollute either directly by mining fossil

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.970
fuels or indirectly through their business
models that would leave it for the attention

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.099
to be on.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:43.849
Did you recycle every milk carton or not?

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:50.110
Are you still using a plastic straw rather
than the significant industrial pollution

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.530
that they contribute to?

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:57.599
So the analysis is the same, but that statistic
100 companies contribute to 71 percent of

00:02:57.599 --> 00:02:58.599
all pollution.

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:02.910
No, it's within fossil fuels within the fossil
fuel industry.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:08.569
Seventy one percent of that pollution comes
from 100 fossil fuel producers.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:15.909
I'm sorry the analysis is the same, but it
would have been stronger had I not cited that.

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:17.030
And I'm going to do better in the future.

